diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist in the network. PC15 (172.18.51.15) and PC16 (172.18.51.16) are both directly connected to switch10 via eth1 and eth2 respectively — the only device between them. A comprehensive inspection at every layer shows healthy operation: both PC ethernet interfaces are in state UP with correctly assigned IPs on the same /24 subnet (172.18.51.0/24); switch10's Linux bridge (br0) has both ports in FORWARDING state with no STP blocking; the forwarding database (FDB) correctly maps each PC's MAC to its physical port; iptables and ebtables on switch10 carry zero rules (all chains ACCEPT); ARP resolution is working in both directions; and bidirectional ICMP testing — 4 packets each way — completed with 0% packet loss and round-trip times of under 1.4 ms. There is no injected fault, no misconfiguration, and no failed hardware on the path between these two machines. The root cause is that the user's ticket describes a connectivity problem that does not currently exist in this lab environment. No corrective action is needed on the network side; the engineers should verify the end-host application configuration (file-sharing service, firewall on the OS, share permissions) if the users continue to experience issues, as that layer is outside the network infrastructure's scope.
